 "notes": "I matched 182 of the 230 stations to an Edwards row. The other 48 get null: 16 observer, fog-signal or foreign stations, and 32 lights or light-vessels that Edwards doesn't list. Several rows are shared by two stations because Edwards prints one '2 lights' entry for both: St Ann's Head (3 stations), Nash, Maidens, Eagle Island, Slyne Head and Hoy Sound.\n\nPlease check:\n(1) Errors in the Edwards CSV:\n- p187r7–r12 (Little Ross to Ardrossan) are numbered from 1 again, so they are Scottish rows, but the country column says ENGLAND—continued.\n- p192r14–r20 are the Channel Islands (numbered 1–7) but are labelled IRELAND--continued.\n- The Isle of Man rows (p187r3–r6) have no numbers.\n- p185r18 (Scarweather) is numbered 9; it should be 89.\n- Bressay's range (p189r1) is 6, probably 16.\n(2) Lowestoft: Edwards has two rows, Lowestoftness and Lowestoft High. I picked the High light (p182r19) on judgment.\n(3) Calf Rock and Dursey: I put 'Dursey Head' (p192r11) on dursey-island and left calf-rock null, because that light was destroyed in 1881.\n(4) Copeland: Edwards describes the old Copeland Island light. By Findlay 1887 the light is at Mew Island with a different character, so p191r6 fits only the station's earlier years.\n(5) Killybegs '2 lights' (p191r17) probably covers St John's Point as well as Rotten Island.\n(6) Characters that disagree with Findlay:\n- Goodwin (North Sand Head) light-vessel: Edwards gives 3 flashes a minute; Findlay 1879 and 1887 both say revolving 1 min.\n- Hartland Point: Edwards gives white and red; Findlay just 'revolving'.\n- Wolf Rock: Edwards gives white and red alternating; Findlay doesn't mention colour.\nThese may be changes or misprints, so check them against the scans.\n(7) Nulls worth confirming:\n- Tees light-vessel, Dee light-vessel, Flintshire Point of Air, Straw Island, Samphire Island, Holy Isle (Lamlash), Portpatrick: not in Edwards. These are minor lights, or for Point of Air, mixed up with the Isle of Man's Ayre 